**Read in full:** [Slip-correction measurements — Kim, Mulholland, Kukuck and Pui](../sources/Slip-Correction-Measurements-Kim.lingua#r_51NCSC0TX3G750ZF72EQ4FGJ49), including its uncertainty analysis; compare the textbook relation in [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0).

The NIST study infers slip behavior from electrical mobility of specified polystyrene-latex particle references while changing pressure. That inference joins size, field and flow geometry, voltage, gas properties and observed response. A small-looking change in a fitted coefficient can be meaningful only relative to these uncertainties and conventions.

Its reported fit uses C=1+Kn[α+βexp(−γ/Kn)] with α=1.165, β=0.483 and γ=0.997 in its convention. These are not the coefficients chosen for the preceding illustrative correlation. Preserve the stated mean-free-path model and measured range when comparing them.

**Work:** Reconstruct the measurement equation and uncertainty contributions from the paper. Compare the two correlations over a common justified range and report the difference. Identify which data constrain the fit and what evidence would be required for a different particle material or pressure regime. Do not claim reproduction of the experiment from merely evaluating its fitted formula.

